==================================================================
DAY 13 - PART A: THEORY
Namespaces and the Imperative vs Declarative Workflow
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHAT IS A NAMESPACE?
-----------------------
A namespace is a virtual folder inside one cluster. It gives:
  - name scoping: "web" in dev and "web" in prod can both exist
  - a boundary for access control (RBAC, Week 5)
  - a boundary for resource quotas (Week 5)
  - a boundary for network policies (Week 5)

It is NOT a hard security or network boundary by itself: pods in
different namespaces can still talk to each other by default.


2. BUILT-IN NAMESPACES
----------------------
  default           where things go if you don't say otherwise
  kube-system       control plane and system add-ons
  kube-public       readable by everyone (rarely used)
  kube-node-lease   node heartbeat objects (Lease) for fast failure
                    detection


3. NAMESPACED vs CLUSTER-SCOPED
-------------------------------
Namespaced:      Pods, Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, Secrets ...
Cluster-scoped:  Nodes, Namespaces, PersistentVolumes,
                 StorageClasses, ClusterRoles ...

  k api-resources --namespaced=true
  k api-resources --namespaced=false


4. WORKING WITH NAMESPACES
--------------------------
  k get pods -n dev               one namespace
  k get pods -A                   all namespaces
  k create namespace dev
  metadata.namespace: dev         in YAML
  k config set-context --current --namespace=dev   change default
  kubens dev                      same thing, shorter

Deleting a namespace deletes EVERYTHING inside it. Be careful.


5. DNS ACROSS NAMESPACES (PREVIEW OF WEEK 3)
--------------------------------------------
A Service "web" in namespace "prod" is reachable as:
  web                         (from inside prod)
  web.prod                    (from any namespace)
  web.prod.svc.cluster.local  (full name)


6. THREE WAYS TO MANAGE OBJECTS
-------------------------------
Imperative commands      k run, k create deploy, k scale, k set image
  + fast, good for learning, exams, emergencies
  - no record, not repeatable

Imperative with files    k create -f, k replace -f, k delete -f
  + files exist
  - you must know if the object already exists

Declarative              k apply -f <file or folder>
  + idempotent, works with Git, supports diff
  + the standard for teams and GitOps
  - must keep files in sync (never mix with "k edit" in production)

Rule of thumb: generate with imperative commands (--dry-run=client
-o yaml), store in Git, deploy with apply.


7. ORGANISING MANIFESTS
-----------------------
A simple layout that scales:

  app/
    base/            deployment.yaml, service.yaml
    dev/             namespace.yaml, overrides
    prod/            namespace.yaml, overrides

You will automate the "overrides" part with Kustomize/Helm in Week 8.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Namespaces scope names, RBAC, quotas, policies; not network.
- -n NAME or -A; set a default with kubens.
- Generate imperatively, store in Git, apply declaratively.
